;; Docs for std/effect.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "effect.bjodoc"). The
;; texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Tail-resumptive effect handlers: declare operations, perform them, and install handlers for an extent.")
  (see defeffect with-handler handler-of)
  (example """
    (import (std prelude))
    (import (std effect))

    (defeffect Console
      (say (-> string void))
      #:default ((say (fun (s) (println s)))))

    (defun (greet who) (say (str "hello " who)))

    (greet "world")                          ; the default: prints it
    (with-handler ((say (fun (s) (ignore s))))
      (greet "nobody"))                      ; says nothing
    """)
  (reference """
    An effect operation is a parameter that holds a function, the
    @strong{handler}. To perform the operation, call it as a function: the
    call reads the parameter and calls the handler. @code{with-handler}
    is @code{parameterize} on the parameters of the operations. Code that
    performs an operation does not know which handler is installed, so a
    caller decides what the operation does. A test, for example, can
    install a handler that collects what the code logs.

    @strong{A handler returns once, or raises.} The handler runs on the
    stack of the code that performs the operation, and no continuation is
    captured. So a handler can return a value, which is resuming the
    operation, or raise an exception, which aborts it. Nothing else is
    possible. Generators, backtracking and resuming an operation two times
    are not possible with these effects, and can not be added to them on
    this backend.

    @strong{What a declaration makes.} For each operation @code{op},
    @code{defeffect} defines two ordinary names in the module:
    @code{op/op}, the parameter, and @code{op}, a function that calls the
    handler. A module that lets other modules handle its operation exports
    both.

    @strong{Colour.} An operation declared @code{(-> ...)} takes
    @code{fun} handlers and can be performed anywhere, also in a
    @code{defun}. An operation declared @code{(-bjo-> ...)} takes
    @code{bjoroutine} handlers and can be performed only from a
    bjoroutine. A @code{fun} is not accepted where a
    @code{(-bjo-> ...)} handler is needed. Prefer @code{(-> ...)}: an
    operation that a @code{defun} performs must be one. An operation that
    must give something that suspends can return a value whose own methods
    can suspend, such as a port.

    @strong{Dependencies.} This module does not import the prelude, so the
    prelude can declare effects of its own. The parameter functions that
    an expansion uses are built in, so the expansion works in any module.
    """))

(:doc unhandled-effect
  (summary "Raises Unhandled for an operation that has no handler.")
  (arg operation "The name of the operation, for the message.")
  (returns "Nothing. It always raises.")
  (raises Unhandled "Always. The message names the operation.")
  (see defeffect)
  (reference """
    The handler of an operation that @code{defeffect} declares with no
    @code{#:default}. The expansion calls it, so it is exported.
    """))

(:doc defeffect
  (summary "Declares the operations of an effect, with their types and optional default handlers.")
  (form (defeffect name operation ...))
  (form (defeffect name operation ... #:default (default ...) #:cold (cold ...) #:adopt (adopt ...)))
  (arg name "The name of the effect. It is documentation only: no definition is made for it.")
  (rest operation """
    @code{(op arrow)}: the name of an operation and its type, which is an
    arrow: @code{(-> ...)} or @code{(-bjo-> ...)}.
    """)
  (rest default """
    @code{(op handler)}: the handler that @code{op} uses when no
    @code{with-handler} installs one. An operation without a default
    raises @code{Bjolang.Runtime.Unhandled} when it is performed.
    """)
  (rest cold """
    An operation that uses a cold parameter. See below.
    """)
  (rest adopt """
    @code{(op fn)}: a function that gets each value that the handler of
    @code{op} returns, before the caller gets it. See below.
    """)
  (see with-handler handler-of unhandled-effect)
  (example """
    (defeffect Log
      (log   (-> string void))
      (audit (-bjo-> string void))
      #:default ((log (fun (s) (println s))))
      #:cold (audit))
    """)
  (reference """
    The @code{#:default}, @code{#:cold} and @code{#:adopt} clauses are each
    optional, and can be in any order. Each operation defines a parameter,
    @code{op/op}, and a function, @code{op}, that calls the handler in
    the parameter. Handlers are installed one operation at a time, with
    @code{with-handler}. No form installs a whole effect.

    @strong{Default.} Give a default when the operation has one sensible
    meaning. Leave it out when a program that runs with no handler must
    fail. The default must have the colour of the operation: a
    @code{bjoroutine} for a @code{(-bjo-> ...)} operation.

    @strong{Cold.} Each operation uses one of the 31 cheap parameter ids of
    the dynamic environment. @code{#:cold} makes the parameter of the
    operation a cold parameter, which uses no id. Use it for an operation
    that is not performed in a loop, so that operations which are
    performed often keep the cheap ids.

    @strong{Adopt.} Use @code{#:adopt} for an operation that returns a
    resource, when a handler can make a resource that nothing owns, such
    as a string port. The function can register the resource on the
    current scope, so that it is released as a real one is. If the
    operation returns the raw material instead, and the caller makes the
    resource, @code{#:adopt} is not necessary. The @code{FS} openers of
    the prelude return a @code{Stream} for this reason.
    """))

(:doc with-handler
  (summary "Runs body with handlers installed for some operations.")
  (form (with-handler ((op handler) ...) body ...))
  (arg op "The name of an operation, as defeffect declared it. Give at least one pair.")
  (arg handler "The handler for that operation. Its colour is the operation's.")
  (rest body "What to run. At least one form. Its last value is the value of the form.")
  (see defeffect handler-of)
  (example """
    (def buf (make-box ""))
    (with-handler ((log (fun (s) (box-set! buf (str (box-ref buf) s)))))
      (run-job))
    """)
  (reference """
    This is @code{parameterize} on the parameters of the operations. The
    bindings are made at the same time, and the earlier handlers come back
    however the body ends. A handler runs on the stack of the code that
    performs the operation. So a handler that raises unwinds that code,
    not the code that installed the handler. In a spawned fiber, that is a
    failure of the child, as any other.

    @strong{A handler frame is not a scope.} @code{with-handler} does not
    wait for the fibers that its body spawns. A spawned fiber keeps the
    handler, because a spawn takes the environment as it is at the spawn.
    But @code{with-handler} can return before the fiber has performed its
    operations:

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    (with-cancel (cancel)
      (with-handler ((log (collect-into buf)))
        (spawn (work)))
      (report buf))   ; too early: the child still writes to buf
    }|

    So put a handler that collects outside the scope that owns the fibers,
    and read what it collected after both have returned.
    @code{parameterize} works the same way.

    @strong{Forwarding.} A handler runs with itself installed. So a handler
    that performs its own operation calls itself, and loops. Use
    @code{handler-of} to get the handler that was installed before. The
    bindings are evaluated before any of them is installed, so the binding
    itself can read it:

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    (with-handler ((log (let ((outer (handler-of log)))
                          (fun (s) (outer (str "[app] " s))))))
      body)
    }|

    @strong{The parameter is found by its spelling.} @code{with-handler}
    makes the name of the parameter from the written name of the
    operation: @code{log} gives @code{op/log}. An operation imported with a
    prefix or a rename has no parameter with that spelling, so
    @code{with-handler} can not find it. Use @code{parameterize} on the
    parameter itself:
    @code{(parameterize ((p:op/log collect)) body)}.

    For the same reason, a macro that writes a @code{with-handler} must
    @code{inject} the name of the operation, as in
    @code{(inject (string->symbol "log"))}. A template renames the names
    that it introduces, and the renamed name gives a parameter that is not
    declared. A name that comes from the input of the macro is not renamed.
    @code{with-fake-fs} in @code{(std simpletest)} is an example.
    """))

(:doc handler-of
  (summary "The handler that is installed for an operation here.")
  (form (handler-of op))
  (arg op "The name of an operation, as defeffect declared it.")
  (see with-handler)
  (reference """
    @code{(parameter-ref op/op)}, with the name of the parameter made as
    @code{with-handler} makes it. Use it in a @code{with-handler} binding
    to forward to the handler that the binding replaces.
    """))
